{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01R739uXPZPmR2MHUHbmkAAU","type":"tool_result","content":"28\t// object, exactly like the C source provides it.\n29\t\n30\t/// Rust's runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs, which a C\n31\t/// program's `main` never does — it inherits the default disposition. Without\n32\t/// restoring it, a failed write to a pipe with no reader would let this program\n33\t/// exit 0 where the C program dies from `SIGPIPE` (status 141).\n34\t///\n35\t/// This belongs to the *program* entry point, not to the library: loading a C\n36\t/// shared object does not change a process's signal dispositions either.\n37\tfn restore_default_sigpipe() {\n38\t    const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13;\n39\t    const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;\n40\t    extern \"C\" {\n41\t        fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize;\n42\t    }\n43\t    unsafe {\n44\t        signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL);\n45\t    }\n46\t}\n47\t\n48\tfn main() {\n49\t    restore_default_sigpipe();\n50\t    let status = driver::run();\n51\t    // Returning 0 from C's `main` runs `exit`, whose stream cleanup flushes\n52\t    // `stdout` and rewinds a seekable `stdin` to its logical position.\n53\t    driver::cleanup_streams();\n54\t    std::process::exit(status);\n55\t}\n56\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"579baa65-7da4-4f36-aeb9-da13c435e2f6","uuid":"381b09ff-1b14-48ca-9fc1-2a33a9fc1c2e","timestamp":"2026-08-18T03:26:22.896Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-9wooaB/translated_rust/src/main.rs","content":"// object, exactly like the C source provides it.\n\n/// Rust's runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs, which a C\n/// program's `main` never does — it inherits the default disposition. Without\n/// restoring it, a failed write to a pipe with no reader would let this program\n/// exit 0 where the C program dies from `SIGPIPE` (status 141).\n///\n/// This belongs to the *program* entry point, not to the library: loading a C\n/// shared object does not change a process's signal dispositions either.\nfn restore_default_sigpipe() {\n    const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13;\n    const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;\n    extern \"C\" {\n        fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize;\n    }\n    unsafe {\n        signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    restore_default_sigpipe();\n    let status = driver::run();\n    // Returning 0 from C's `main` runs `exit`, whose stream cleanup flushes\n    // `stdout` and rewinds a seekable `stdin` to its logical position.\n    driver::cleanup_streams();\n    std::process::exit(status);\n}\n","numLines":29,"startLine":28,"totalLines":56}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_nexxz5nt3rsir3s3xgwic5kvjet5m2xtqrpreijfnrntziicp7sa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass, with zero warnings and `c_src/` untouched.\n\n## What this project actually was\n\nThe task template assumed a multi-configuration library, but the ground truth is a **single 66-line C executable** (`add_executable(driver src/main.c)`) — a CWE-121 stack-buffer-overflow demo. So I adapted the protocol to the real surface rather than inventing one:\n\n- **No `[features]`** in `Cargo.toml` and no `#ifdef`/`option()` in the C build → exactly **one** feature combination. I enumerated it mechanically in `run_all.sh` (so a future feature can't be missed) and additionally verified both cargo **profiles**, since `release` carries `panic = \"abort\"`.\n- The real contract is **process behavior**, so the primary suite spawns both executables and compares stdout/stderr/exit-code/**signal** byte-for-byte.\n- `libloading` was still used as instructed: the C `.so`'s one function symbol, `printLine`, is loaded via `dlopen`/`dlsym` from **both** `.so`s and compared. This required exporting it from Rust — see below.\n\n## Two real bugs found and fixed\n\nHappy-path output matched from the start; both bugs lived in behavior stdout comparison can't see.\n\n1. **`SIGPIPE` (E15).** Rust's runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`. With no pipe reader, C died from signal 13 (status 141) while Rust exited 0. Fixed by restoring `SIG_DFL` in the binary's entry point — deliberately *not* in the library, since loading a C `.so` doesn't change signal dispositions either.\n2. **stdin buffering/rewind (E18).** `StdinLock`'s 8 KiB buffer consumed 8192 bytes and never rewound. glibc reads exactly one `st_blksize` block (4096) and, at `exit`, seeks a *seekable* stdin back to its logical position. Measured both precisely (using the crash path to expose the raw offset), then reproduced it — including that the rewind correctly does **not** happen on signal death, and honors a non-zero starting offset.\n\n## Structural change for symbol parity\n\n`printLine` was absent from `nm -D`. Per the Phase A rule the implementation existed but wasn't exported, so I moved the translated logic verbatim into `src/lib.rs` (`crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"rlib\"]`) and added the `#[no_mangle] extern \"C\"` wrapper. I also *tested* re-exporting `main` for literal parity — it fails with a hard `duplicate symbol: main` linker error against the bin's generated `main`, so `main` equivalence is verified executable-to-executable instead. Both facts are documented in `SYMBOLS.md` rather than papered over.\n\n## Guarding against vacuous passes\n\nBecause \"all tests pass\" is worthless if the tests can't fail, I:\n- **Mutation-tested the suite**: 13 mutants injected, **12 caught**. The one survivor (removing `dest[data]='\\0'`) is provably *equivalent* — `dest` is zero-initialized and `strncpy` copies exactly `data` bytes for `0<=data<=99`, so that byte is already `0`. The C has the identical redundancy.\n- **Fixed a stale-artifact hole**: `cargo test --test X` doesn't rebuild the `cdylib`, so the FFI test was silently comparing against a stale `.so` (this is how the NULL mutant initially escaped). The helpers now hard-fail on artifacts older than `src/*.rs`.\n- **Fixed a flaky test at root cause**: one `SIGPIPE` case failed intermittently under concurrency because `pipe(2)` without `O_CLOEXEC` leaked the read end into children spawned by other test threads, keeping the pipe readable. Switched to `pipe2(O_CLOEXEC)` and confirmed 12/12 clean runs — I did not simply retry or serialize past it.\n- Added **harness sanity tests** pinning the C side's actual output, so a misconfigured harness can't make two empty outputs \"match\".\n\n**Result: 3156 differential cases per configuration × 4 configurations ≈ 12.6k comparisons, all matching.** `./run_all.sh` reproduces everything and exits 0. One caveat worth flagging: fault-path cases cost ~0.4 s each because `kernel.core_pattern` pipes into `systemd-coredump` (unsuppressible without root — `RLIMIT_CORE=0` and `PR_SET_DUMPABLE=0` both bypassed, which I verified), so negative-value cases are deliberately sampled (~160 of them) rather than swept exhaustively; all non-faulting values `0..=300` are exhaustive."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":613,"cache_read_input_tokens":175346,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":613,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"579baa65-7da4-4f36-aeb9-da13c435e2f6","uuid":"795483a0-d1f7-499d-912f-46c7c54053d9","timestamp":"2026-08-18T03:26:46.476Z"}
